    decision "what a persona serves" \
        chose="anchor the three personas to FRAME.260819.01, the weakest anchor in the vocabulary" \
        over="a `persona` entity kind, so a persona could serve the phases it owns" \
        over="anchoring each persona to the invariants its skills teach" \
        over="retiring them, on the grounds that the graph names actors and not roles" \
        because="a persona is not a capability, an invariant or a slice. It is a statement about \
            WHO may attest what, and the reason this plugin has personas at all is the \
            trust-transfer problem — the same engineer cannot self-approve, because an artifact \
            looks identical whether the reviewer was a different mind or the same one wearing a \
            second hat. That is S2, and S2 belongs to the frame. A `persona` kind was rejected as \
            three records' worth of vocabulary for three records; anchoring to invariants was \
            rejected because a persona does not enforce anything" \
        falsified-by="a fourth persona, or a persona that needs to say something the frame cannot \
            hold — at which point three surfaces pointing at one frame stops being an argument and \
            becomes the dumping ground it currently resembles" \
        seal="fnv1a64:997fb5b9e20e1e74" {
        amendment "the falsifier fired, and not the way it was written" at="2026-08-22T00:00:00Z" \
            by="agent:principal-engineer" \
            note=#"""
                TS.260821.08 reverses the conclusion and keeps the reasoning.

                The prediction was a FOURTH persona. What happened is that the frame went from
                one surface to four inside the same iteration, and the tell is structural rather
                than numeric. The frame is the ROOT — every surface descends from it by
                construction — so `serves` naming it is true of everything and discriminates
                nothing. A target true by construction cannot discriminate, and a field that
                cannot discriminate is a dumping ground when it holds ONE thing. It was already
                wrong at one.

                The rejected alternative "a persona entity kind" was rejected for being three
                records' worth of vocabulary for three records. That was the mistake. `role` earns
                its place not by anchoring three files but by making "the same engineer cannot
                self-approve" computable — the rule this plugin states most confidently and, until
                now, enforced least.

                `frame` is removed from the serves vocabulary. The fix is not a rule that detects
                the pile: no-dumping-grounds forbids the NAMES utils, helpers, common, and never
                measures whether a folder became a junk drawer. Anti-dumping works by making the
                dumping ground unnameable.
                """#
    }

